# Showing and Running Code
.code, .play and friends
9 Oct 2026

Ada Example
ada@example.com

## A whole file

`.code code/hello/main.go` shows a file as it is:

.code code/hello/main.go

## Part of a file

An address picks the lines to show. Lines ending in `OMIT` are left out:

.code code/fib/main.go /START OMIT/,/END OMIT/

A regular expression alone picks a single line:

.code code/fib/main.go /^func main/

## Highlighting

Lines ending in `// HLstep` are highlighted when the command ends in `HLstep`:

.code code/fib/main.go /^func fib/,/^}/ HLstep

Each word marks its own group of lines. This file also has `// HLmain`:

.code code/fib/main.go /^func main/,/^}/ HLmain

## Line numbers and editing

.code -numbers code/fib/main.go /^func main/,/^}/

`-edit` lets the audience change the code on the slide:

.code -edit code/hello/main.go

## Running Go

`.play` runs the program on the machine running present. Click Run, or press shift+enter:

.play code/hello/main.go

: Run present with -play=false to turn off running programs.

## Running only part of the source

Only the selected lines are shown, but the whole file is run:

.play -edit code/pipeline/main.go /^func main/,/^}/

## Several files

A [txtar](https://pkg.go.dev/golang.org/x/tools/txtar) archive holds several files; without a go.mod, one is made.

.fontsize 0.9

.play code/multi.txtar

## Compile errors

Only `main` is shown, but the errors' line numbers are those of the file, the one in `count` too:

.play -numbers code/_broken/main.go /^func main/,/^}/

## Running Python

A `.py` file runs with `python3`:

.play -edit code/primes.py /^print/

## Running JavaScript and TypeScript

`.js` files run with `node`, `.ts` files too once `tsc` has checked and compiled them:

.play code/fizzbuzz.js

.play code/shapes.ts /^function area/,/^}/

## Other languages

A program starting with `#!` runs with that interpreter:

.play code/system.sh
